recap (generated Recap.thy) — implementation plan
==================================================

status: planned (2026-07-14)
2026-07-14). NOT scheduled into a wave yet — it depends on nothing
unbuilt (mcp_tool_command is DONE), so it can land whenever a wave
opens for it.

the question this plan answers first: scala or ML?
---------------------------------------------------

ML ONLY. no scala.

the engine is Isabelle/ML under every option — enumerating a theory's
own facts, printing their statements as re-parseable source, auditing
oracles/axioms are all prover-side operations with no scala analogue.
the only real choice is whether to ALSO add a scala builtin row, and
the answer is no:

- as an ML tool (`mcp_tool recap = run \<open>...\<close>` with params),
  registration goes through MCP_Tool.declare and the existing registry
  serves it over tools/list for free — zero scala lines. run_tool
  already returns (status, TEXT), which is exactly recap's output
  contract (theory source out, client writes the file).
- as a scala builtin it would STILL need an ML mirror row (see
  plans/builtin_activation) and STILL cross to ML for the actual work.
  the only thing scala buys is "listed even when the context does not
  import the recap theory". an end-of-session action on a HOL session
  that already imports MCP-HOL does not need that.

so: a new theory MCP_Recap.thy in the MCP-HOL tree (next to
MCP_Repl.thy), an ML structure MCP_Recap, one `mcp_tool` declaration.
if a later wave wants recap available in contexts that don't import it,
promoting it to a builtin is a mechanical move (mirror row + table row,
same ML entry point) — do not pre-pay for it.

spec refinement: recap targets THEORIES, not repls
---------------------------------------------------

the spec's mechanism sketch says "session/repl id in". a repl id is
NOT a sound input, and this is the plan's load-bearing correction.

a generated Recap.thy is a FILE that imports on-disk theories. it can
only `by (fact Scheduler.no_starvation)` a fact that exists in a
theory the recap imports. facts that live only in a repl's transient
theory (steps taken in-session, never written back) exist in no file:
a recap naming them cannot build, and one that silently skips them
recaps nothing. the repl is the workspace, the theory is the record.

therefore the input contract is:

  recap {theories: "Scheduler Tasks", name?: "Recap"} -> theory TEXT

theory names, resolved with Thy_Info.get_theory (the idiom entities /
find_theorems_theory already use, MCP_Repl.thy:101,159) — an explicit
param, NOT the ambient tool_scope designation: recap is about a named
target and the caller should not have to tool_scope_set to name it.

a repl-shaped affordance still exists, and it is the honest one: the
agent's end-of-session loop is (1) client persists the repl's steps
into the theory file, (2) load_theory to check it, (3) recap on the
theory name. recap over an unpersisted repl is refused with that
sentence, not approximated. (a future `repl` param could recap a
repl's PENDING facts as commented-out "not yet persisted" entries —
deferred, and only as a nudge toward step 1.)

design priority: the artifact is SOURCE, not prose
---------------------------------------------------

the deliverable is a theory file an Isabelle developer AUDITS BY EYE
and checks with `isabelle build`. it is emphatically NOT an agent's
prose account of what it proved and how that relates to the goal —
prose of that kind is unauditable by construction and is the thing
recap exists to replace. consequences, binding on every step below:

- EVERY claim in the file is a checked Isar construct: the theorems
  are `by (fact ...)`, the definitions/constants go through @{const}
  and @{thm} antiquotations (which fail at build time if the entity is
  gone), the theory list is the `imports` clause. nothing asserts in
  free text what a construct could assert.
- the generated source is written for a HUMAN READER: statements
  restated in full (not `@{thm foo}` handwaving), one theorem per
  entry, sections per theory, stable order. it should read like a
  theory a careful person wrote, because a careful person has to read
  it.
- the ONLY natural-language content permitted is (a) the linking prose
  attached to a requirement (see the spec's requirements-traceability
  section — and that prose is the HUMAN's, quoted from the declared
  requirement, not the agent's) and (b) the trust audit's plain
  statements of fact ("rests on: Pure.skip_proof"). no summary, no
  narrative, no "how this relates to the goal".
- a recap that cannot restate something honestly comments it out with
  the reason. it never paraphrases its way past a failure.

soundness vs completeness (why "completely automatic" is justified)
--------------------------------------------------------------------

the human's verification step is `isabelle build` on the generated
Recap.thy. under that contract, imperfect term printing costs
COMPLETENESS, never SOUNDNESS:

- a statement printed too generally, or drifted, makes `by (fact ...)`
  fail to close the goal -> the build FAILS -> a human looks. loud.
- a false claim can never build, because the recap proves everything
  by reference to a real theorem.
- a missing entry (filtered out) yields a recap that under-claims.

so the tool cannot vouch for something untrue; the worst it does is
fail to vouch, visibly. the honesty machinery (below) closes the one
remaining hole — a recap that builds green while resting on `sorry`.

what the recap contains
-----------------------

for each target theory T (in dependency order):

  theory Recap
    imports Scheduler Tasks
  begin

  section \<open>Scheduler\<close>

  text \<open>definitions formalized: @{const eventually_runs}, ...\<close>

  theorem recap_no_starvation:
    "\<And>t. t \<in> tasks \<Longrightarrow> eventually_runs t"
    by (fact Scheduler.no_starvation)

  ...
  end

plus a TRUST AUDIT section, emitted as text (and as a build-visible
comment): oracles behind each restated theorem (Thm_Deps.all_oracles),
`sorry` (the skip_proof oracle) called out by name, and axioms
introduced by the target theories (Theory.all_axioms_of T minus its
parents'). a recap that rests on a skipped proof SAYS SO in the file
the human reads. entries whose statement fails the round-trip check
(below) are emitted COMMENTED OUT with the reason plus an @{thm}
antiquotation fallback — degraded, never silently dropped.

mechanism
---------

1. fact enumeration: Facts.dest_static over Global_Theory.facts_of T
   with the parents' facts_of as the exclusion bases — the spec's
   "diff against the imports", which Facts.dest_static does natively.
2. fact FILTER: package noise (foo.simps, foo.induct from datatype /
   fun) must not enter the recap. Name_Space.is_concealed alone
   UNDER-FILTERS (those names are user-visible). the kind tag
   (Thm.get_tags -> Markup.kindN, theoremK/lemmaK/corollaryK vs what
   packages set) is the likely discriminator — see A2, this is
   determined empirically, not assumed.
3. RESTATEMENT (the make-or-break step, see A1): print Thm.prop_of thm
   as source the recap theory can re-parse. two candidate shapes:
     (a) un-schematize (Var -> Free) + `theorem name: "..."`;
     (b) keep schematics verbatim + `schematic_goal name: "?x ..."`.
   (b) prints the stored prop with no rewrite and may round-trip more
   faithfully; (a) reads better for a human. the `fact` method
   instantiates the THM's schematics against the goal, so (a) closes
   too — which one closes RELIABLY across type-class constraints,
   binders and custom notation is A1's experiment, not a decision made
   here. printing runs with markup off and (probably) show_types on —
   the round-trip decides.
4. round-trip PRE-FLIGHT, in-process, no build. TWO dimensions, both
   required — an emitted entry is `theorem X: "<stmt>" by (fact N)`,
   so the build needs the STATEMENT to re-parse AND the NAME N to
   resolve:
   - statement: Syntax.read_prop in T's context on the printed string,
     then Pattern.matches against the original prop.
   - name: emit the EXTERNED name (Facts.extern against T's fact
     space — the name a user would type), not the raw internal long
     name. because the recap IMPORTS T, T re-exports its facts under
     that name, so it resolves; the failure mode is emitting the wrong
     name FORM. check it in-process by looking the emitted name back
     up in T's fact space and confirming it hits the same thm.
   entries failing EITHER check are the commented-out ones. with both,
   the output is trustworthy before the client ever writes a file;
   with only the first it would be statement-trustworthy and could
   still fail the build on a name.
5. trust audit: Thm_Deps.all_oracles on the restated theorems;
   Theory.all_axioms_of diffed against parents.

   (APIs verified 2026-07-14 against the bundled Isabelle2025-2:
    Facts.dest_static : bool -> T list -> T -> (string * thm list) list
      — the exclusion-bases argument is native, Pure/facts.ML:40;
    Thm_Deps.all_oracles : thm list -> Proofterm.oracle list
      — Pure/thm_deps.ML:10;
    Theory.all_axioms_of : theory -> (string * term) list
      — Pure/theory.ML:25. note all_axioms_of, NOT axioms_of.)
6. assemble the text: header (imports = the target theory names as
   written), one section per theory, the audit section, `end`.

name/argument mapping across the layers
----------------------------------------

  mcp        recap {theories, name?}
  bridge     MCP.run_tool -> registry row "recap" (ml_run form)
  engine     MCP_Recap.recap : string list -> string (theory text)

implementation steps
--------------------

1. MCP_Recap.thy skeleton + enumeration (steps 1-2 above): facts of T
   minus parents, filtered. [ml unit, MCP-HOL tests: a fixture theory
   with a datatype, a fun, two lemmas and a definition -> exactly the
   two lemmas]
2. restatement + round-trip pre-flight (steps 3-4). the fork in A1 is
   settled HERE, by the test, before any text assembly. [ml unit over
   a corpus of statement shapes]
3. trust audit (step 5). [ml unit: a sorry'd lemma is flagged; an
   axiomatization is listed]
4. text assembly + the `mcp_tool recap = run ...` declaration with the
   theories/name params. [ml unit: golden text for the fixture theory]
5. bridge: recap over MCP.run_tool in a live session returns text.
   [mcp_test -b]
6. THE REAL TEST — build the generated recap: take the fixture
   theory's recap text, write it to a temp dir with a ROOT, run
   `isabelle build`, expect green; then mutate the fixture (rename a
   theorem, weaken a statement) and expect the build to FAIL. this is
   the only test that exercises the human's actual verification step.
   [e2e]
7. close-out: spec section updated (theory-not-repl refinement folded
   back), CHANGELOG entry, plans/README checkbox.

ASSUMPTIONS -> tests   (ranked by risk)
----------------------------------------

- A1 RESTATEMENT ROUND-TRIPS [highest risk — the whole tool rests on
  it]. a printed statement re-parses in the target context to a prop
  that `by (fact ...)` closes, AND the emitted fact NAME resolves in
  a context importing T (the externed name, per step 4 — a statement
  that re-parses under a name that doesn't resolve still fails the
  build, so the pre-flight must check both or the commented-out/live
  decision is wrong). corpus must span: schematic vars,
  type-class-constrained polymorphic statements, binders, custom
  notation/syntax from the target theory, `\<And>`/`\<Longrightarrow>` mixtures,
  and a definition-derived equation. the test asserts the round-trip
  (read_prop + Pattern.matches) AND, for a sample, that the assembled
  theory builds (step 6). settles the (a)/(b) fork of step 3.
  [ml unit + e2e]
- A2 the FACT FILTER discriminates user-stated theorems from package
  output. enumerate on a theory with datatype + fun + definition +
  lemmas and confirm the chosen predicate empirically — is_concealed
  is known insufficient; whether the kind tag suffices is UNVERIFIED.
  [ml unit, or MCP_Heap_Suite if a heap is needed]
- A3 the trust-audit APIs actually flag what they must: Thm_Deps
  .all_oracles reports the skip_proof oracle for a `sorry`'d lemma
  under the session's normal proof-recording settings (NOT only with
  full proof terms — verify), and Theory.all_axioms_of minus parents
  yields exactly an axiomatization's axioms. [ml unit]
- A4 IMPORT-NAME RESOLUTION: the theory names recap writes into the
  `imports` clause resolve from the directory the client writes
  Recap.thy into (session-qualified vs base names). get this wrong and
  every recap fails to build for a boring reason. [e2e, step 6]
- A5 recap of a repl is REFUSED, not approximated (the spec
  refinement, pinned as behavior). [bridge]
